We also examined which mechanisms are being used to achieve grievance and redress among the community female, male, and youth FGDs (@fig-bs_grievance_mechanisms). As can be seen, the main mechanism include community meetings, mediation by traditional leaders and formal grievance and redress committees. The focus on local solutions is a positive finding.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Mechanisms used for grievance and redress in CFMGs, as reported by community female, male, and youth FGDs. Bars show the number of FGDs reporting each mechanism.](CFMG-BS_files/figure-html/fig-bs_grievance_mechanisms-1.png){#fig-bs_grievance_mechanisms width=960}
:::
:::


### Impact on poverty

We asked the community female, male, and youth FGDs to identify the ways in which CFM has had an impact on poverty in their communities (@fig-bs_poverty_impact). Increase in household incomes was the most commonly stated impact on poverty followed by increased nutrition and food security. However, it is interesting to note the number of different types of responses that were given.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Reported impacts of CFM on poverty, as identified by community female, male, and youth FGDs. Bars show the number of FGDs reporting each impact.](CFMG-BS_files/figure-html/fig-bs_poverty_impact-1.png){#fig-bs_poverty_impact width=960}
:::
:::


::: return-to-summary
[← Return to Summary](Summary.html#equity-and-inclusion)
:::

### Barriers to poorer community members

We asked the community female, male, and youth FGDs to identify the barriers that prevent poorer community members from benefiting from CFM (@fig-bs_poverty_barriers). Most often mentioned was "Limited information". However, more concerning were responses like, "Excluded from decisions", "Unequal benefit distribution" and "No trust in leadership". 


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Barriers preventing poorer community members from benefiting from CFM, as reported by community female, male, and youth FGDs. Word size is proportional to the number of FGDs reporting each barrier.](CFMG-BS_files/figure-html/fig-bs_poverty_barriers-1.png){#fig-bs_poverty_barriers width=960}
:::
:::


### Addressing challenges

We asked the community female, male, and youth FGDs to suggest solutions to address the barriers facing poorer community members in benefiting from CFM (@fig-bs_solutions). Far and away the most popular solution for addressing these barriers was community meetings. 


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Suggested solutions to address barriers preventing poorer community members from benefiting from CFM, as reported by community female, male, and youth FGDs. Bars show the number of FGDs reporting each solution.](CFMG-BS_files/figure-html/fig-bs_solutions-1.png){#fig-bs_solutions width=960}
:::
:::


## Equity and inclusion index

To summarise the overall equity and inclusion performance of each CFMG, we constructed an equity and inclusion score (0–5) by summing five binary indicators reported by FGD groups — equitable benefit sharing, women benefiting from CFM, women participating in decision making, transparency in benefit sharing, and existence of a grievance and redress mechanism — each scored as 1 (positive outcome reported) or 0 (no positive outcome). The distribution of equity and inclusion scores across CFMGs is shown in @fig-bs_index_dist (Appendix III). We then modelled this score using a linear mixed model with FGD type and the governance index as predictors, and random intercepts for province and CFA within province, to test whether governance and FGD type were significant predictors of overall equity and inclusion performance (@tbl-bs_index_model, @fig-bs_index).




::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Distribution of mean equity and inclusion scores across CFMGs (0–5). Each bar represents the number of CFMGs with that mean score, averaged across all FGD types. The score sums five binary indicators: equitable benefit sharing, women benefiting from CFM, women participating in decision making, transparency in benefit sharing, and existence of a grievance and redress mechanism.](CFMG-BS_files/figure-html/fig-bs_index_dist-1.png){#fig-bs_index_dist width=672}
:::
:::



